@* Slider's Overview, written by hand after NumberField's: showing the value, saving one (previewed
   in each mode), following it live on an interactive page, empty values and states.
   Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A slider is a native range input. It suits a value the user judges by eye, such as a volume. It posts with the form, and it moves with the arrow keys, Page Up and Page Down, and Home and End, with <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">JavaScript off</Anchor> too. When the exact number matters, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/number-field">NumberField</Anchor>, or both side by side.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Showing the value</Heading>
<TextBlock>A slider can show its value in a tooltip over the thumb. To show it while the thumb is hovered, focused or dragged, set <Code>ValueTooltip</Code> to <Code>SliderTooltip.Active</Code>; to show it all the time, <Code>SliderTooltip.Always</Code>. To label the ends of the track with the lowest and highest values, set <Code>ShowEnds</Code>. To set how values read, such as <Code>"{0}%"</Code>, set <Code>ValueFormat</Code>.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>The ends are 0 and 100 by default. To change them, set <Code>[Range]</Code> on the property, or <Code>Min</Code> and <Code>Max</Code> on the slider. To set the increments, set <Code>Step</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="slider-value" />

<Heading Level="2">Marks</Heading>
<TextBlock>Marks point out values on the track, such as a recommended setting. Set <Code>Marks</Code> to the values, each with an optional label, as <Code>new SliderMark(49, "49°C")</Code>. To put a dot at every step instead, set <Code>ShowStepMarks</Code>. The thumb still moves by <Code>Step</Code>, and marks show with JavaScript off.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="slider-marks" />

<Heading Level="2">Saving a value</Heading>
<TextBlock>A slider binds like any field. The value posts as a number with the form.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="slider-budget" />
<TextBlock>The browser keeps the value on a step between the ends, so a form post from the page is always in range. A crafted form post can send any number. The validator checks <Code>[Range]</Code> on the server, but not the step, so round the value yourself if it must be a multiple. Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Following the value live</Heading>
<TextBlock>By default, the bound value changes when the user lets go of the thumb. On interactive pages, a live slider updates it as the thumb moves instead, for something on the page that follows the value. Set <Code>Live</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="slider-live" />
<TextBlock>Each move is a round trip to the server, so set <Code>Live</Code> only when something follows the value. On static pages, the value posts with the form either way.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Vertical</Heading>
<TextBlock>A slider can stand upright, as in a mixer or a levels panel. Set <Code>Orientation</Code> to <Code>Orientation.Vertical</Code>. The lowest value is at the bottom, and Up raises it. The value shows under the track. To change the track's length from 10rem, set <Code>Height</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="slider-vertical" />

<Heading Level="2">Empty values</Heading>
<TextBlock>A range input always has a value. Bound to a nullable property that's null, the slider shows the thumb at the midpoint, snapped to the step, and posts that value. When "not set" must stay possible, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/number-field">NumberField</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes and disabled</Heading>
<TextBlock>To line the slider up with other fields, set <Code>Size</Code>. For a value the user can't change, set <Code>Disabled</Code>; a disabled slider doesn't post.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>There's no <Code>ReadOnly</Code>, because HTML has no read-only range input. In a read-only <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>, the slider is disabled, and a hidden field posts its value.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="slider-states" />
<TextBlock>Before saving a disabled value from a form post, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#disabled-fields-and-form-posts">Disabled fields and form posts</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        @* 0 to 100, the value over the thumb while it's moved. *@
        <Slider @bind-Value="Model!.Volume" ValueTooltip="SliderTooltip.Active" />

        @* Its own ends and steps, read as a percentage. *@
        <Slider @bind-Value="Model!.Discount" Min="0" Max="50" Step="5"
                ValueFormat="{0}%" ShowEnds ShowStepMarks />

        @* Upright, the lowest value at the bottom. *@
        <Slider @bind-Value="Model!.Microphone" Orientation="Orientation.Vertical" />
        """;
}
